  <MedicalNote />

  <p>Your Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) is the number of calories your body burns at complete rest just to stay alive. Even if you spent an entire day lying still, your body would still spend this energy keeping your heart beating, your lungs breathing, your brain working, and your cells repairing and regenerating. BMR is the single largest component of your daily calorie expenditure — typically 60–70% of the total — making it the foundation for figuring out how much you should eat.</p>

  <ArticleImage src="/assets/images/articles/basal-metabolism.svg" alt="Illustration of a resting metabolism with a flame inside a body outline, representing calories burned at rest." />

  <h2>Why BMR matters</h2>
  <p>Understanding your BMR helps you make informed decisions about your calorie intake. Once you know your BMR, you can estimate your full daily needs (your <a href="/guides/whats-my-tdee/">TDEE</a>) and set targets for weight loss, maintenance, or weight gain. Without knowing your BMR, it is difficult to determine whether your current calorie intake is appropriate for your goals.</p>
  <p>For example, if your BMR is 1,400 kcal/day and you eat 1,200 kcal/day, you are consuming fewer calories than your body needs at rest. Over time, this can lead to muscle loss, hormonal disruption, and a slower metabolism — not sustainable fat loss. On the other hand, eating above your TDEE consistently will lead to weight gain, which may be intentional if your goal is to build muscle.</p>

  <h2>BMR vs RMR — what is the difference?</h2>
  <p>Basal Metabolic Rate (BMR) and Resting Metabolic Rate (RMR) are closely related but measured under slightly different conditions:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>BMR</strong> is measured after a full night of sleep and 12 hours of fasting, in a thermoneutral environment, with the subject completely at rest and awake. These strict conditions ensure the measurement reflects only the energy needed for basic life functions.</li>
    <li><strong>RMR</strong> is measured under less strict conditions — no fasting requirement, and the subject may have been lightly active earlier in the day. As a result, RMR is typically 5–10% higher than BMR.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>In everyday nutrition and fitness contexts, the two terms are used interchangeably. Our <a href="/">BMR</a> estimates RMR using the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, which is the most widely accepted predictive formula for the general population.</p>

  <h2>How to calculate BMR</h2>
  <p>The widely recommended predictive formula is the Mifflin-St Jeor equation, published in 1990 using modern metabolic data:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Men:</strong> BMR = 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) − 5 × age + 5</li>
    <li><strong>Women:</strong> BMR = 10 × weight(kg) + 6.25 × height(cm) − 5 × age − 161</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The formula requires your weight in kilograms, height in centimeters, and age in years. If you prefer imperial units, convert first: 1 kg = 2.205 lb, 1 cm = 0.394 in. Our <a href="/">BMR</a> handles the math instantly and supports both metric and imperial inputs.</p>
  <p>For a deeper comparison of equations, see our <a href="/guides/bmr-equations/">BMR equations guide</a>.</p>

