  <h2>How to use BMR for weight goals</h2>
  <p>Your BMR is the starting point for any weight goal. Here is how to use it:</p>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Calculate your BMR</strong> using the <a href="/">BMR</a>.</li>
    <li><strong>Estimate your TDEE</strong> by multiplying your BMR by an activity factor (1.2 for sedentary, 1.375 for lightly active, 1.55 for moderately active, 1.725 for very active).</li>
    <li><strong>For weight loss:</strong> Subtract 250–500 calories from TDEE for a moderate, sustainable deficit. The <a href="/guides/calorie-deficit-guide/">calorie deficit guide</a> has detailed instructions.</li>
    <li><strong>For weight gain:</strong> Add 250–500 calories to TDEE for a lean bulk. See the <a href="/guides/calorie-surplus-guide/">calorie surplus guide</a> for best practices.</li>
    <li><strong>For maintenance:</strong> Eat at your TDEE and recalculate every 4–6 weeks as your body composition changes.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>Always use the <a href="/">Calorie Calculator</a> to ensure your protein intake is adequate, especially during weight loss, to preserve muscle mass.</p>

  <h2>Common misconceptions about BMR</h2>
  <p><strong>Myth: "Skinny people have higher BMRs."</strong> Not necessarily. Larger bodies, even with higher body fat, often have higher BMRs because there is more total tissue to maintain. Muscle mass matters more than body weight alone.</p>
  <p><strong>Myth: "You can dramatically boost your BMR with supplements."</strong> Most metabolism-boosting supplements have minimal real-world impact. Caffeine may raise BMR slightly and temporarily, but the effect is small and diminishes with tolerance.</p>
  <p><strong>Myth: "Eating more meals speeds up your metabolism."</strong> The thermic effect of food depends on total calorie and protein intake, not meal frequency. Eating three meals or six meals produces the same total thermic effect for the same calories.</p>
  <p><strong>Myth: "Your BMR is fixed and unchangeable."</strong> While your BMR has a genetic baseline, it is not fixed. Building muscle through resistance training, changing body composition, and improving hormonal health can all shift your BMR over time.</p>

  <h2>Try the Calorie Calculator</h2>
  <p>Now that you understand what BMR is and how it works, get your personal number with the <a href="/">BMR</a>. It uses the Mifflin-St Jeor equation and takes only a minute. Combine your BMR result with the <a href="/tdee-calculator/">TDEE calculator</a> to find your full daily calorie needs.</p>

